tanding"], "rationale": "The polling average puts Peltola up roughly two points, with the strongest recent surveys (Alaska Survey Research, Fabrizio Ward) showing her ahead by four to six, and both Kalshi and PredictIt price her near 71%. Sullivan's incumbency and Alaska's Republican lean keep this close, but the weight of recent evidence favors a modest Democratic edge.", "watch": "The New York Times reporting on turmoil inside Peltola's campaign, if it deepens, could shift the race back toward Sullivan.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Dan Sullivan (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Alaska's Senate race pits incumbent Republican Dan Sullivan against Democrat Mary Peltola in a contest most raters call a tossup, though several lean toward a Peltola flip. Polling since August has consistently shown Peltola narrowly ahead, with September surveys from Alaska Survey Research, Fabrizio Ward, and a Republican-sponsored poll averaging a small Democratic lead. Peltola has also dramatically outraised Sullivan, though Republican outside groups have heavily targeted her with attack spending.\n\nPrediction markets give Peltola about a 71% chance, suggesting investors see her ahead of what polls alone imply. Still, Sullivan benefits from incumbency in a state that leans Republican, and late-September reporting on internal chaos in Peltola's campaign introduces real downside risk. The race remains genuinely competitive, with a modest but not decisive Democratic edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5747, "margin": 0.87}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6955, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6995, "margin": 2.5}]}